<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iranian Green Movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2009">2009</a>–<a href="/years/2010">2010</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Green Movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iranian Green Movement was a wave of mass protests that followed the disputed presidential election of June 2009, in which Mahmoud Ahmadinejad was declared the winner. Supporters of the defeated reformist candidates Mir-Hossein Mousavi and Mehdi Karroubi demonstrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and other cities. The authorities crushed the protests, killing dozens and arresting thousands, and placed Mousavi and Karroubi under house arrest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iranian-green-movement.jpg" alt="Iranian Green Movement" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solid_green_flag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Mahmoud Ahmadinejad, a conservative and former mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, had been president since 2005. Human Rights Watch reported that basic human rights protection had deteriorated under his first term, and The Guardian reported his popularity falling, especially in the cities and among the young. In the election of 12 June 2009 his strongest opponent was the former prime minister Mir-Hossein Mousavi, whose campaign colour was green.</p><p>Mousavi claimed victory, while Ahmadinejad's office almost at once announced that the president had won about two-thirds of the votes. Mousavi and his supporters held the result to be fraudulent and accused the interior minister, Sadegh Mahsouli, an ally of Ahmadinejad, of distorting the count. Green became the sign of those demanding that the election be annulled, and Where is my vote? became their motto.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Clashes began on 13 June, which Al Jazeera English called the biggest unrest since the 1979 revolution. On 15 June Mousavi addressed a rally around Azadi Tower whose size was put at anything from hundreds of thousands to three million, and on 17 June more than half a million marched silently from Haft-e-Tir Square to Vali Asr Square. Over 170 people, among them reformist politicians such as Behzad Nabavi and Mohsen Mirdamadi, were arrested on the first weekend, and some 500 by 17 June. By that day the Huffington Post reported 32 dead.</p><p>Protests continued through the autumn on national occasions such as Quds Day on 18 September and the anniversary of the US Embassy takeover on 4 November. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a> the protests turned more violent: hundreds were arrested and at least 15 people died, among them Mousavi's nephew Seyed Ali Mousavi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under arrests, street attacks and restrictions on the internet and text messages, the demonstrations dwindled within a year, and Mousavi and Mehdi Karroubi were placed under house arrest. Mousavi and other reformist leaders formed the Green Path of Hope, calling it a path to avoid the need for official permission. On 14 February 2011 the largest Green demonstrations in more than a year broke out, and groups linked to the movement later supported Hassan Rouhani's campaign for the presidency.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iranian Green Movement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Reformist periods under Mohammad Khatami alternated with crackdowns, including on the <b>Green Movement</b> of 2009 and the 'Woman, Life, Freedom' protests of 2022.</blockquote><a href="/state/islamic-republic-of-iran">Islamic Republic of Iran</a></li></ul></section>
